Subject: Seal a light frame only against its own chunk

What this does
  Seal a light frame only against its own chunk.

Why we need it
  When that push opened a new chunk, the record fill sealed `regvm_cur` again and a frame in the previous chunk against the new chunk's base, so a recursion alternating a light function with one that has a `try` ran out of arena at depth 141 instead of about 5000.

What it adds
  `regvm_light_seal` returns when the frame lies outside the current chunk; that frame is already covered by its chunk's `saved_top`, because every handler seals before it pushes.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  light-only and record-only depths unchanged
diff --git a/quickjs.c b/quickjs.c
index d962a8e..1b9398e 100644
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -26478,12 +26478,19 @@ regvm_light_alloc(JSRuntime *rt, JSRegRun *f, JSFunctionBytecode *cb)
 static inline void regvm_light_seal(JSRuntime *rt, JSRegRun *lf)
 {
     if (lf != NULL && lf->sf == NULL) {
-        size_t top = (size_t)((uint8_t *)lf->fr - rt->regvm_frames)
-                   + lf->b->regvm_end_off;
-
-        /* A light frame's record-shaped end can sit past this chunk's end (its
-           light shape fits, its end_off does not). Clamp so a later chunk pop
-           does not restore a top past the chunk. */
+        size_t top;
+
+        /* Only a frame in the current chunk can be sealed against the current
+           top. A light frame left in the previous chunk when a push opened a
+           new one is already covered by its `saved_top`; measuring it against
+           the new chunk would mark that chunk full. */
+        if ((uint8_t *)lf < rt->regvm_frames
+            || (uint8_t *)lf >= rt->regvm_chunk_end)
+            return;
+        top = (size_t)((uint8_t *)lf->fr - rt->regvm_frames)
+            + lf->b->regvm_end_off;
+        /* The record-shaped end can pass the chunk end when only the light
+           shape fits. */
         if (top > rt->regvm_frames_size)
             top = rt->regvm_frames_size;
         if (top > rt->regvm_frames_top)
